\chapter{Rendering a Record for Reading}
\label{render}

A recorded run reaches a reader as [[git show]] output: forty
hexadecimal digits, an author line, a unified diff, and a message whose
every line is indented by four spaces because Git indents messages.
That is a fine format for a program and a poor one for the person the
log is kept for.
A first-year student looking at their own afternoon should see what
they ran, whether it worked, what they had changed since the last time,
and what the terminal said---and should see the exception message
before they see the run identifier.

This chapter turns one record into a \emph{card}: a small ordered list
of lines, each labelled with what kind of thing it is.
Nothing here decides what the lines look like on a particular screen.
A line carries a [[kind]]---[[removed]], [[err]], [[section]]---and the
two surfaces that display records, the curses viewer and the batch
writer (\cref{viewer}), turn that kind into a curses attribute or an
ANSI escape at the moment of drawing.

\section{Why the display layer is a module of its own}

Splitting rendering out is what keeps colour out of the log.
This module imports [[learnlog.commits]] and the standard library and
nothing else; it takes no repository argument, opens no file, and
returns values rather than writing them.
A module with no repository cannot write to one, so no escape sequence
and no friendly phrase can end up in a commit message by accident:
what is stored stays the transcript the student's program produced, and
everything in this chapter happens after it has been read back.
That is a stronger guarantee than a rule saying \enquote{do not
colour the log}, because it is enforced by the import list rather than
by discipline.

The second reason is that one description of a record then serves both
surfaces.
The viewer draws with [[addstr]] and the batch writer prints with
[[print]]; if each built its own card they would drift, and a tutorial
that tells the student what they will see would be right about only one
of them.

\ltnote{%
  The order of this chapter is the order in which the pieces stop
  depending on each other, not the order a reader meets them on screen.
  The diff and the terminal session are explained first because they
  are self-contained line-to-kind rules, and the card is explained last
  because it is assembled from them.  Presenting the card first would
  mean forward-referencing two state machines to explain a list of
  section headings.%
}

\section{Code overview}

The module is a pipeline with a wide middle: a handful of classifiers
that each turn one kind of text into styled lines, and two entry points
that assemble them.
[[record_lines]] builds the friendly card, [[raw_lines]] builds the
unchanged [[git show]] text with colour added, and the remaining
public functions serve the two surfaces: [[use_color]] and
[[ansi_lines]] for the batch writer, [[curses_styles]] for the viewer.

