\section{A line and its style}
\label{sec:styled-line}

A rendered line is at most two pieces of text.
The terminal-session lines have a stream prefix---[[stdout: ]],
[[stdin: ]], [[stderr: ]]---and everything after it is what the
student's program actually said.
We want the eye to land on the program's own words, so the prefix is
drawn faded while the text keeps its colour and weight.

Two segments rather than a general list of spans, because nothing in a
record needs a third: the diff keeps its [[+]] and [[-]] markers inside
the coloured text exactly as [[git diff]] does, and every other line is
one piece.  Two segments also keep both consumers trivial---two
[[addstr]] calls, or two escape-wrapped substrings.

<<render types>>=
StyledLine = collections.namedtuple(
    "StyledLine", ["text", "kind", "prefix"], defaults=("",),
)
@

\subsection{The vocabulary of kinds}

Each line names a \emph{kind}, and the kinds are a closed vocabulary:
[[STYLES]] below has a row for every one of them, and a test checks
that nothing this module emits falls outside the table.
Naming them as constants rather than writing the strings at each site
means a mistyped kind raises [[NameError]] the first time that line is
built, instead of producing a line that silently loses its colour.

