\subsection{The style table}

One table describes both surfaces, because the alternative---a curses
table here and an ANSI table there---is two lists that have to be
edited together and can therefore disagree.

The curses half is a description rather than an attribute, because
attributes do not exist until [[initscr]] has run and because
[[curses]] is unimportable on a Windows machine without
[[windows-curses]] (\cref{viewer}).
A module that named [[curses.A_BOLD]] at the top level would fail to
import for a student who only ever runs [[learnlog play -b]].
[[curses_styles]] turns these descriptions into attributes later,
inside curses.

We use only the eight basic colours against the terminal's own
background.
That is the portable subset: 256-colour pairs and explicit backgrounds
are where PDCurses, [[TERM=linux]] consoles and colour-blind-friendly
terminal themes start to differ from each other.
PDCurses also ignores [[A_DIM]] on some terminals, so dimming must
never be the only thing distinguishing two kinds---which is why the
faded stream prefixes keep their [[stdout: ]] text and the diff keeps
its [[+]] and [[-]] markers.  Read without any colour at all, a card
still says everything it meant to.

<<render types>>=
