\section{Making text safe to draw}
\label{sec:sanitise}

A captured transcript is whatever the student's program printed, and a
program prints things a terminal grid cannot hold.
A tab is the dangerous one: [[addstr]] expands it against the window's
own tab stops, and a line that ends past the right edge makes [[addstr]]
raise [[curses.error]], which costs the \emph{whole} line, not the
overflowing part.  Expanding tabs ourselves puts the width under our
control before curses sees it.

A carriage return is the common one.  The capture layer splits its
buffer on [[\r]] as well as [[\n]] (\cref{capture}), because progress
bars redraw a line by returning to its start; a stray [[\r]] still
reaching us here would make a terminal overwrite the line it just drew.
We drop it rather than replace it, because a carriage return carries no
text---it is a cursor movement.

Everything else in the control range becomes a question mark, so it
occupies one visible cell and says that something was there.
[[unicodedata]] answers \enquote{is this a control character} for the
C1 range as well as C0, which matters because escape sequences whose
introducer survived a truncation can start there.

<<render constants>>=
TAB_WIDTH = 4
CONTROL_REPLACEMENT = "?"
<<render functions>>=
def sanitise(text):
    """Return ``text`` with tabs expanded and controls removed.

    Tabs become spaces, carriage returns are dropped, and every
    other control character becomes ``?``.
    """
    expanded = text.expandtabs(TAB_WIDTH)
    return "".join(
        CONTROL_REPLACEMENT
        if unicodedata.category(character) == "Cc"
        else character
        for character in expanded
        if character != "\r"
    )
@

Let's verify each of the three treatments, including that ordinary
text and non-ASCII letters pass through untouched:

<<render test functions>>=
def test_sanitise_expands_tabs():
  assert sanitise("a\tb") == "a   b"
  assert sanitise("\tx") == "    x"

def test_sanitise_drops_carriage_returns():
  assert sanitise("50%\r100%") == "50%100%"

def test_sanitise_replaces_other_controls():
  assert sanitise("a\x00b\x1bc") == "a?b?c"
  assert sanitise("a\x7fb") == "a?b"

def test_sanitise_keeps_ordinary_text():
  text = "k\u00f6r igen! \u2014 ja"
  assert sanitise(text) == text
@


\section{Reading the parts of a record}
\label{sec:record-parts}

[[split_commit_detail]] (\cref{sec:split-commit-detail}) hands back the
three parts of a [[git show]]: the header lines, the message with
Git's four-space indent already removed, and the diff lines.
That is everything the card needs, and it is the only splitting this
module does.

It reads the order Git produces---header, message, diff---and
[[get_commit_detail]] can hand back a different one, with the diff
moved in front of the message so that a reader meeting the raw text
sees the code changes first (\cref{commits}).
Both views here ask for Git's own order, with [[reorder=False]], for
two different reasons.
The card composes its own layout out of the parts and would have to
undo the move before it could tell message from diff.
The raw view (\cref{sec:raw-lines}) wants Git's order because that is
what a record \emph{is}: the diff-first arrangement was a convenience
for reading the text as text (\cref{commits}), and a view whose promise
is to show what was recorded should not show a rearrangement of it.

The default stays [[reorder=True]], because the callers that predate
this chapter are the ones the convenience was written for.  Keeping the
choice in the reader is also what lets this module take one layout and
know it: nothing below has to recognise a rearranged [[git show]] or
guess where a moved message begins.

\subsection{The run blocks of a message}

A commit message holds zero or more run blocks separated by
[[BLOCK_SEP]] (\cref{sec:block-sep}).
Zero is not an error: the initial commit's message is
[[learnlog: initial commit]], and a merge written by [[learnlog pull]]
records no run either.
We recognise a run block by the same [[Run: ]] prefix the rest of the
package uses, so a message that is not a run record simply yields an
empty list and the card falls back to describing the commit itself.

<<render functions>>=
def run_blocks(message):
    """Return the run blocks of a commit message, in order.

    A message that records no run---the initial commit, a
    merge---yields an empty list.
    """
    blocks = []
    for block in message.split(BLOCK_SEP):
        first_line = block.split("\n", 1)[0]
        if first_line.startswith(RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX):
            blocks.append(block)
    return blocks
@

Let's verify both halves of that: a message with two runs, and one
that records none.

<<render test functions>>=
def test_run_blocks_splits_and_ignores_non_runs():
  message = (
    "Run: hello.py (none)\nExit-Code: 0"
    + BLOCK_SEP +
    "Run: hello.py (none)\nExit-Code: 1"
  )
  assert len(run_blocks(message)) == 2
  assert run_blocks("learnlog: initial commit") == []
@


\subsection{The command a subject names}

Several decisions depend on \emph{what} was run: whether to call the
record a program run, a look back through the log, or a restore.
The subject line carries it, and one function pulls it out so the
predicates in \cref{commits} can be applied to it rather than to a
string match on the subject.
That matters because \enquote{is this a review run} is defined by a
list of subcommands the recorder also consults (\cref{sec:run-visibility});
matching [[Run: learnlog play]] here instead would be a second,
private copy of that list.

<<render functions>>=
def run_command(subject):
    """Return the command a ``Run:`` subject names, else None."""
    if not subject.startswith(RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX):
        return None
    return subject[len(RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX):]
@

Let's verify that a run subject yields its command and the initial
commit's subject yields nothing:

<<render test functions>>=
def test_run_command_reads_the_subject():
  assert run_command("Run: hello.py (none)") == "hello.py (none)"
  assert run_command("learnlog: initial commit") is None
@


\section{The diff}
\label{sec:classify-diff}

A unified diff cannot be classified one line at a time, because the
same characters mean different things depending on where in the diff
they appear.
[[--- a/hello.py]] near the top of a file's diff is the name of the old
file; the identical text inside a hunk is a \emph{deleted line} whose
content happened to be [[-- a/hello.py]].
A renderer that matched on [[--- ]] alone would paint a student's
deleted line as plumbing and hide it.

So the classifier is a small state machine with three states.
Outside any file we have not met a [[diff --git]] line yet.  Inside a
file's \emph{preamble}---from [[diff --git]] up to the first
[[@@]]---the metadata spellings are meaningful.  Inside a \emph{hunk}
they are not, and every line is body text whose first character is its
marker.

\subsection{One headline per file}

Git spends up to seven lines saying which file a diff is about, in a
vocabulary aimed at another Git.
A student needs one line: the name, plus a word if the file is new,
gone, or renamed.  The catch is that the word arrives \emph{after} the
name---[[new file mode]] and [[rename from]] come below the
[[diff --git]] line---so the headline cannot be emitted when the name
is read.  We keep the file pending and flush it at the first line that
leaves the preamble: a hunk, a \enquote{binary files differ}, the next
file, or the end of the diff.

<<render constants>>=
DIFF_FILE_PREFIX = "diff --git "
HUNK_PREFIX = "@@"
BINARY_PREFIX = "Binary files "
NO_NEWLINE_PREFIX = "\\ "
NEW_FILE_MODE = "new file mode"
DELETED_FILE_MODE = "deleted file mode"
RENAME_FROM = "rename from "
NEW_FILE_SUFFIX = " (new file)"
DELETED_SUFFIX = " (deleted)"
RENAMED_SUFFIX = " (renamed from {})"
BINARY_NOTE = "(binary file)"
PREAMBLE_PREFIXES = (
    "index ",
    "old mode ",
    "new mode ",
    "similarity index ",
    "rename to ",
    "--- ",
    "+++ ",
)
@

Returning [[False]] for a line the preamble does not recognise is what
keeps a malformed or future diff readable: the line falls through to
the body rules and is shown, rather than being swallowed as metadata.

<<render functions>>=
def _consume_preamble(pending, line):
    """Fold one preamble line into the pending file headline.

    Returns false for a line that is not preamble metadata.
    """
    if line.startswith(NEW_FILE_MODE):
        pending["suffix"] = NEW_FILE_SUFFIX
    elif line.startswith(DELETED_FILE_MODE):
        pending["suffix"] = DELETED_SUFFIX
    elif line.startswith(RENAME_FROM):
        pending["suffix"] = RENAMED_SUFFIX.format(
            line[len(RENAME_FROM):],
        )
    elif not line.startswith(PREAMBLE_PREFIXES):
        return False
    return True
@

The two paths on a [[diff --git]] line are the same name twice, once
under [[a/]] and once under [[b/]], and that repetition is the only
reliable way to read a name containing a space---which
[[my program.py]] is, on plenty of student machines.
Splitting on the first [[ b/]] is right for that name and wrong for a
name that itself contains one, so we first test whether the line is two
equal halves and cut it exactly in the middle.
A rename breaks the symmetry on purpose---the halves are different
names---so there we fall back to splitting at the first [[ b/]], which
is what Git's own porcelain does and is wrong only for a name that
contains that sequence itself.

<<render functions>>=
def _begin_file(paths):
    """Start a pending file headline from ``diff --git`` paths."""
    return {"name": _diff_file_name(paths), "suffix": ""}
<<render functions>>=
def _diff_file_name(paths):
    """The displayed name of the file ``paths`` describes."""
    half = (len(paths) - len("a/ b/")) // 2
    if half > 0 and paths.startswith("a/") and \
            paths[2:2 + half] == paths[-half:]:
        return paths[2:2 + half]
    if " b/" in paths:
        return paths.split(" b/", 1)[1]
    return paths
@

Flushing a pending file does nothing in raw mode, where the original
[[diff --git]] line was emitted when it was read.  That is what lets
the flush be called unconditionally at each of the four places the
preamble can end, instead of each of them asking which view it is in.

<<render functions>>=
def _file_headline(pending, raw):
    """The headline lines for a pending file, if any."""
    if raw or pending is None:
        return []
    text = pending["name"] + pending["suffix"]
    return [StyledLine(FILE_INDENT + sanitise(text), KIND_FILE)]
@

\subsection{Lines inside a hunk}

Inside a hunk the marker character decides, and it stays part of the
coloured text rather than being stripped.
That is what [[git diff]] does, and it is what makes a diff readable
where colour is unavailable---a printout, a pipe into [[less]] without
[[-R]], a terminal with [[NO_COLOR]] set.  Colour becomes the fast
path, never the only one.

<<render functions>>=
def _diff_body_kind(line):
    """The kind of one line inside a hunk."""
    if line.startswith(HUNK_PREFIX):
        return KIND_HUNK
    if line.startswith(NO_NEWLINE_PREFIX):
        return KIND_NOTE
    if line.startswith("+"):
        return KIND_ADDED
    if line.startswith("-"):
        return KIND_REMOVED
    return KIND_CONTEXT
@

\subsection{Putting the diff together}

The [[raw]] parameter is what lets one classifier serve both views.
The friendly view drops the preamble, indents the body under its file
headline, and sanitises the text; the raw view keeps every line exactly
as Git wrote it and only attaches a kind.
Keeping them in one function rather than two is what stops the two
views from disagreeing about which lines are additions.

<<render constants>>=
FILE_INDENT = "  "
DIFF_INDENT = "    "
<<render functions>>=
def classify_diff(lines, *, raw=False):
    """Classify unified diff lines into styled lines.

    With ``raw`` false the metadata around each file becomes a
    single headline and the body is indented beneath it; with
    ``raw`` true every line is kept verbatim and only labelled.
    """
    result = []
    pending = None
    in_hunk = False
    for line in lines:
        <<start a file when the diff names one>>
        <<fold the preamble into one headline>>
        <<classify a line of the diff body>>
    result.extend(_file_headline(pending, raw))
    return result
@

A [[diff --git]] line both ends the previous file and begins the next,
so it flushes before it starts.

<<start a file when the diff names one>>=
if line.startswith(DIFF_FILE_PREFIX):
    result.extend(_file_headline(pending, raw))
    pending = _begin_file(line[len(DIFF_FILE_PREFIX):])
    in_hunk = False
    if raw:
        result.append(StyledLine(line, KIND_DIM))
    continue
@

A hunk header leaves the preamble without being consumed by it: it
flushes the headline, switches state, and then falls through to the
body rules, which know that [[@@]] is a hunk header.  Writing it that
way means the second and later hunks of a file---which arrive in the
hunk state and never reach this branch---are classified by the same
line of code as the first.

A binary file has no hunks at all, so it would otherwise leave a name
with nothing under it.  We say so in one dim line instead.

<<fold the preamble into one headline>>=
if pending is not None and not in_hunk:
    if line.startswith(HUNK_PREFIX):
        result.extend(_file_headline(pending, raw))
        pending = None
        in_hunk = True
    elif line.startswith(BINARY_PREFIX):
        result.extend(_file_headline(pending, raw))
        pending = None
        result.append(
            StyledLine(line, KIND_DIM) if raw
            else StyledLine(
                DIFF_INDENT + BINARY_NOTE, KIND_NOTE,
            )
        )
        continue
    elif _consume_preamble(pending, line):
        if raw:
            result.append(StyledLine(line, KIND_DIM))
        continue
@

An empty line is dropped in the friendly view because a unified diff
has no empty content lines---every line inside a hunk carries a marker,
so an empty one is the blank that separates the diff from whatever
[[git show]] put next to it, rather than anything the student wrote.

<<classify a line of the diff body>>=
if raw:
    result.append(StyledLine(line, _diff_body_kind(line)))
elif line:
    result.append(StyledLine(
        DIFF_INDENT + sanitise(line), _diff_body_kind(line),
    ))
@

Let's verify the state machine on the case that motivates it: a deleted
line that looks exactly like a file header.

<<render test functions>>=
def test_classify_diff_file_headers_only_in_preamble():
  """A --- line inside a hunk is a deleted line."""
  lines = [
    "diff --git a/notes.md b/notes.md",
    "index 1111111..2222222 100644",
    "--- a/notes.md",
    "+++ b/notes.md",
    "@@ -1,2 +1,2 @@",
    "--- a/notes.md",
    "+++ b/notes.md",
  ]
  styled = classify_diff(lines)
  kinds = [line.kind for line in styled]
  assert kinds == ["file", "hunk", "removed", "added"]
  assert styled[0].text == "  notes.md"
  assert styled[2].text == "    --- a/notes.md"
@

Every preamble spelling must be recognised, and each must yield exactly
one headline with the right word after the name:

<<render test functions>>=
def test_classify_diff_one_headline_per_file():
  lines = [
    "diff --git a/new.py b/new.py",
    "new file mode 100644",
    "index 0000000..e69de29",
    "--- /dev/null",
    "+++ b/new.py",
    "@@ -0,0 +1 @@",
    "+print(1)",
    "diff --git a/gone.py b/gone.py",
    "deleted file mode 100644",
    "index e69de29..0000000",
    "@@ -1 +0,0 @@",
    "-print(2)",
    "diff --git a/old.py b/renamed.py",
    "similarity index 100%",
    "rename from old.py",
    "rename to renamed.py",
    "diff --git a/mode.py b/mode.py",
    "old mode 100644",
    "new mode 100755",
    "diff --git a/logo.png b/logo.png",
    "index 3333333..4444444 100644",
    "Binary files a/logo.png and b/logo.png differ",
  ]
  styled = classify_diff(lines)
  headlines = [
    line.text for line in styled if line.kind == "file"
  ]
  assert headlines == [
    "  new.py (new file)",
    "  gone.py (deleted)",
    "  renamed.py (renamed from old.py)",
    "  mode.py",
    "  logo.png",
  ]
  assert "    (binary file)" in [line.text for line in styled]
  assert not any(
    "index " in line.text for line in styled
  )
@

A file whose name contains a space still comes back whole, because the
two halves of the [[diff --git]] line agree:

<<render test functions>>=
def test_classify_diff_reads_names_with_spaces():
  styled = classify_diff([
    "diff --git a/my program.py b/my program.py",
    "@@ -1 +1 @@",
    "-x",
    "+y",
  ])
  assert styled[0].text == "  my program.py"
@

A file whose last line has no trailing newline gets a marker of Git's
own, which is a note about the file rather than a line of it:

<<render test functions>>=
def test_classify_diff_marks_missing_newline():
  styled = classify_diff([
    "diff --git a/a.txt b/a.txt",
    "@@ -1 +1 @@",
    "-old",
    "\\ No newline at end of file",
    "+new",
  ])
  kinds = [line.kind for line in styled]
  assert kinds == ["file", "hunk", "removed", "note", "added"]
