\section{The terminal session}
\label{sec:classify-io}

A captured log is a transcript in which every line says which stream it
came from (\cref{capture}).
The prefixes are learnlog's words, not the program's, and on screen
they should read as a margin: present, so the student can tell an
answer they typed from output they read, but never competing with the
text beside them.
That is what the two-segment line in \cref{sec:styled-line} is for---
the prefix goes in the faded segment and the program's own text keeps
the colour of its stream.

The indent goes into the prefix rather than the text, so that the
segment boundary falls exactly where learnlog stops speaking and the
program starts.
A line that names no stream has no such boundary to mark, so its indent
is part of its text.

<<render constants>>=
IO_INDENT = "  "
IO_KINDS = {
    "stdout": KIND_OUT,
    "stderr": KIND_ERR,
    "stdin": KIND_INPUT,
}
@

Only a prefix at the very start of the line is learnlog's.
A program that prints the string [[stdin: ]] has that recorded as
[[stdout: stdin: ]]\ldots, and splitting off more than the first prefix
would rewrite the program's output into a stream it never used.

<<render functions>>=
def _io_prefix(line):
    """Split one log line at its stream prefix.

    Returns ``(prefix, text, kind)``, where ``prefix`` keeps any
    leading indent, or None when the line names no stream.
    """
    body = line.lstrip(" ")
    indent = line[:len(line) - len(body)]
    for prefix, stream in IO_STREAM_PREFIXES:
        if body.startswith(prefix):
            return (indent + prefix,
                    body[len(prefix):],
                    IO_KINDS[stream])
    return None
@

An unprefixed line is not a malformed one.
When a run overruns the log's size cap, the capture layer appends a
bracketed note of its own with no prefix, and that is the one line a
reader must not lose: it is the log saying that it is incomplete
(\cref{sec:io-notes}).
It is shown whole and dim---learnlog talking about the run, rather than
either stream of it.

<<render functions>>=
def classify_io(io_log):
    """Classify a captured I/O log into styled lines."""
    if not io_log:
        return []
    result = []
    for line in io_log.split("\n"):
        split = _io_prefix(line)
        if split is None:
            result.append(StyledLine(
                IO_INDENT + sanitise(line), KIND_NOTE,
            ))
            continue
        prefix, text, kind = split
        result.append(StyledLine(
            sanitise(text), kind, IO_INDENT + prefix,
        ))
    return result
@

Let's verify the three streams, the split point, and that a program
which prints something that looks like a prefix keeps it:

<<render test functions>>=
def test_classify_io_splits_each_stream():
  styled = classify_io(
    "stdout: Name? \n"
    "stdin: Daniel\n"
    "stderr: NameError: name 'nme' is not defined"
  )
  assert [line.kind for line in styled] == ["out", "input", "err"]
  assert styled[0].prefix == "  stdout: "
  assert styled[0].text == "Name? "
  assert styled[1].prefix == "  stdin: "
  assert styled[2].text == \
      "NameError: name 'nme' is not defined"

def test_classify_io_keeps_an_inner_prefix():
  styled = classify_io("stdout: stdin: type your name")
  assert styled[0].prefix == "  stdout: "
  assert styled[0].text == "stdin: type your name"
@

The truncation marker has no prefix and must survive whole:

<<render test functions>>=
def test_classify_io_keeps_an_unprefixed_line():
  marker = "[learnlog: I/O log truncated at 1000000 bytes]"
  styled = classify_io("stdout: hi\n" + marker)
  assert styled[1].kind == "note"
  assert styled[1].prefix == ""
  assert styled[1].text == "  " + marker

def test_classify_io_without_a_log():
  assert classify_io(None) == []
  assert classify_io("") == []
