\section{What the record is called}
\label{sec:record-title}

The headline answers \enquote{what is this?} in the student's own
terms.
Three records need saying differently from the rest: the commit that
started the project, a look back through the log, and a restore of an
earlier version.
Those last two are decided by the predicates in \cref{commits} applied
to the command, not by matching the subject text, so this module cannot
drift from the list of subcommands the recorder itself consults
(\cref{sec:run-visibility}).

The arguments placeholder disappears here.  [[Run: hello.py (none)]]
spells \enquote{no arguments} as [[(none)]] because the trailer format
needs a token where arguments would be; a headline does not, and
[[hello.py (none)]] would read to a beginner as if [[(none)]] were part
of what they ran.

<<render constants>>=
NO_ARGUMENTS_SUFFIX = " (none)"
INITIAL_SUBJECT = "learnlog: initial commit"
INITIAL_TITLE = "Project created"
REVIEW_TITLE = "You looked back through your log"
CHECKOUT_TITLE = "You restored the files from an earlier run"
<<render functions>>=
def record_title(subject):
    """Return the headline for a record's subject line.

    A program run yields the command it ran; learnlog's own
    records and the initial commit yield a sentence describing
    what happened.
    """
    command = run_command(subject)
    if command is None:
        if subject == INITIAL_SUBJECT:
            return INITIAL_TITLE
        return sanitise(subject)
    if is_review_command(command):
        return REVIEW_TITLE
    if is_checkout_command(command):
        return CHECKOUT_TITLE
    return sanitise(command.removesuffix(NO_ARGUMENTS_SUFFIX))
@

Let's verify each of the four answers, including that arguments the
student really passed are kept while the placeholder is not:

<<render test functions>>=
def test_record_title_of_a_program_run():
  assert record_title("Run: hello.py (none)") == "hello.py"
  assert record_title("Run: hello.py alpha beta") == \
      "hello.py alpha beta"

def test_record_title_of_learnlogs_own_records():
  assert record_title("learnlog: initial commit") == \
      "Project created"
  assert record_title("Run: learnlog play") == \
      "You looked back through your log"
  assert record_title("Run: learnlog list --all") == \
      "You looked back through your log"
  assert record_title("Run: learnlog checkout abc1234") == \
      "You restored the files from an earlier run"
@


\section{The card}
\label{sec:record-lines}

A card tells the story of one record in the order a student asks about
it: what did I run, how did it go, what had I changed, and what did the
terminal say.
Nothing that only Git needs appears---no run identifier, no author, no
blob hashes, no section markers from the trailer format.
Those are still in the log, and \cref{sec:raw-lines} shows them on
request; what the default view leaves out is what a beginner cannot act
on.

<<render constants>>=
BLANK_LINE = StyledLine("", KIND_PLAIN)
META_SEPARATOR = " \u00b7 "
SHORT_HASH_LEN = 7
MERGE_HEADER_PREFIX = "Merge:"
@

\subsection{When it happened, and for how long}

The date is written out rather than shown as an ISO timestamp.
A student recognises \enquote{Wed 9 Sep 2026 10:04} as an afternoon
they remember; \enquote{2026-09-09T10:04:00} has to be decoded first,
and the decoding is the part that makes a log feel like a machine's
record rather than their own.

The day-of-month is assembled rather than formatted with [[%-d]].
That flag is a glibc extension: it strips the leading zero on Linux and
macOS, and raises [[ValueError]] on Windows, whose C library has never
had it.  Learnlog ships a Windows dependency precisely because students
run it there (\cref{viewer}), so a format string that works on the
teacher's laptop and crashes on half the class is not a saving.

<<render functions>>=
def _timestamp_text(run, commit):
    """The record's start time, written out for a reader."""
    raw = (run or {}).get("start_time") or commit.get("date", "")
    stamp = _parse_iso(raw)
    if stamp is None:
        return sanitise(raw)
    return (stamp.strftime("%a ") + str(stamp.day)
            + stamp.strftime(" %b %Y %H:%M"))
@

A run whose trailer never arrived has no end time, and reporting a
duration of zero for it would be a claim about a run learnlog did not
see finish.  We omit the phrase instead; the outcome line says what
happened.

<<render functions>>=
def _duration_text(run):
    """How long the run took, or ``""`` when it never finished."""
    if run is None:
        return ""
    start = _parse_iso(run.get("start_time"))
    end = _parse_iso(run.get("end_time"))
    if start is None or end is None:
        return ""
    elapsed = (end - start).total_seconds()
    return "took " + format_elapsed(elapsed)
@

The position within the log rides on the same line.
Batch output has no header bar to carry it, and a student scrolling a
long playback needs to know how far in they are; the viewer's own
header repeats it, which costs one short phrase and keeps a single
call producing what both surfaces show.

<<render functions>>=
def _when_text(run, commit, index, total):
    """The date, duration and position line of a card."""
    parts = [_timestamp_text(run, commit)]
    duration = _duration_text(run)
    if duration:
        parts.append(duration)
    parts.append(f"{index + 1} of {total}")
    return META_SEPARATOR.join(parts)
@

\subsection{The gap and the tags}

These two belong to the commit rather than to any run in it, so a
commit holding several runs shows them once, above the first.
The wordings are fixed by what already exists: playback has printed
[[Time since last run:]] and [[Tags:]] since before there were cards
(\cref{viewer}), the tutorials tell students to look for them, and a
rendering change is no reason for a student's notes to stop matching
their screen.

<<render functions>>=
def _commit_meta(elapsed, tags):
    """The pause before this record, and its tags."""
    lines = []
    if elapsed is not None:
        lines.append(StyledLine(
            f"Time since last run: {format_elapsed(elapsed)}",
            KIND_META,
        ))
    if tags:
        lines.append(StyledLine(
            "Tags: " + ", ".join(sanitise(tag) for tag in tags),
            KIND_META,
        ))
    return lines
@

\subsection{How it went}

The outcome repeats the reasoning of [[classify_run_block]]
(\cref{sec:classify-run-outcomes}) but says more than it does.
That classifier answers a filter, which needs three verdicts; a reader
needs to know which of the several ways a run can end actually
happened, and what the program said on the way out.
A traceback is the most useful thing on the card, so its last line goes
straight into the sentence: a beginner who sees
[[NameError: name 'nme' is not defined]] beside the diff that
introduced [[nme]] has everything needed to fix it.

[[KeyboardInterrupt]] is separated out because it is not a failure at
all---it is the student pressing Ctrl-C---and colouring it red would
teach them to fear their own decision to stop a program.
A missing exit code is likewise not a failure: the trailer never
arrived, so nobody knows how the run ended (\cref{sec:classify-run-outcomes}).

<<render constants>>=
INTERRUPT_EXCEPTION = "KeyboardInterrupt"
<<render functions>>=
def _outcome_line(run):
    """The ``Result:`` line for a parsed run block."""
    exception, message = parse_exception_line(
        run.get("exception_info"),
    )
    if exception == INTERRUPT_EXCEPTION:
        return StyledLine("Result: stopped with Ctrl-C", KIND_WARN)
    if exception:
        detail = f"{exception}: {message}" if message else exception
        return StyledLine(
            f"Result: it crashed \u2014 {sanitise(detail)}",
            KIND_BAD,
        )
    exit_code = run.get("exit_code")
    if exit_code is None:
        return StyledLine(
            "Result: learnlog never saw this run finish", KIND_WARN,
        )
    if exit_code == "0":
        return StyledLine("Result: it worked", KIND_OK)
    return StyledLine(
        f"Result: stopped with exit code {sanitise(exit_code)}",
        KIND_BAD,
    )
@

For learnlog's own records the successful outcome is not news.
\enquote{You looked back through your log} followed by \enquote{it
worked} says nothing a reader wanted; what is worth knowing is how
many runs were looked at, or that a restore happened at all.
A learnlog run that \emph{failed} is worth showing, though, since a
crash in the tool is exactly the thing a student cannot diagnose
without seeing it.

<<render functions>>=
def _outcome_lines(subject, run):
    """The outcome line, when it tells the reader anything."""
    line = _outcome_line(run)
    command = run_command(subject) or ""
    learnlogs_own = (is_review_command(command)
                     or is_checkout_command(command))
    if learnlogs_own and line.kind == KIND_OK:
        return []
    return [line]
@

A review run's card says what the student went and looked at.
The [[Viewed:]] lines themselves stay out of it: they are forty-digit
hashes, and the record that matters is that the looking happened and
how much of it there was.
The card is reached through [[--all]] or through a milestone the
student tagged (\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}), so it is read by someone
who asked to see it, and it still has to read well on its own.

<<render functions>>=
def _review_meta(run):
    """How many records a review run displayed."""
    if not is_review_run(run):
        return []
    count = len(run.get("viewed") or ())
    return [StyledLine(
        f"Looked at {count} run{'' if count == 1 else 's'}",
        KIND_META,
    )]
@

\subsection{The head of a card}

The head is the same three things for every record---what it was, when
it happened, how it went---and the commit-level meta is passed in
rather than computed here, because a commit holding several runs shows
the pause and the tags above the first of them only.

<<render functions>>=
def _head_lines(commit, subject, run, index, total, extra_meta):
    """Title, timing and outcome of one record."""
    lines = [
        StyledLine(_headline(subject), KIND_TITLE),
        StyledLine(_when_text(run, commit, index, total), KIND_META),
    ]
    lines.extend(extra_meta)
    if run is not None:
        lines.extend(_review_meta(run))
        lines.extend(_outcome_lines(subject, run))
    return lines
@

[[record_title]] answers what the record \emph{is}, and for a program
run that answer is the command---which needs a verb in front of it to
become a sentence.
learnlog's own records already are sentences, so they are used as they
stand.  Keeping the split here rather than inside [[record_title]] is
what lets that function stay usable by anything wanting the plain name
of what ran.

<<render functions>>=
def _headline(subject):
    """The card's first line for ``subject``."""
    title = record_title(subject)
    command = run_command(subject)
    if command is None or is_review_command(command) \
            or is_checkout_command(command):
        return title
    return f"You ran {title}"
@

\subsection{The two sections}

Every run record gets a code-changes section, including the runs where
nothing changed.
Saying \enquote{nothing changed since your last run} is the point:
a beginner who re-runs a program expecting different output needs to be
told, in the same place they would have read a diff, that the program
is the one they ran before.
A section that appeared only sometimes could not carry that message,
and the tutorials would have nothing stable to point at.

The initial commit and a merge get the same section under different
words.  A merge brought files in from another copy of the log, so
\enquote{nothing changed since your last run} would be false; the
initial commit predates every run, so it has no \enquote{last run} to
compare with.

<<render constants>>=
CHANGES_HEADING = "Code changes since your last run"
NOTHING_CHANGED = "(nothing changed since your last run)"
MERGE_HEADING = "Code changes from the copy you merged"
MERGED_NOTE = "(merged another copy of this log)"
INITIAL_HEADING = "Files when logging started"
EMPTY_PROJECT = "(the project was empty)"
SESSION_HEADING = "Terminal session"
<<render functions>>=
def _changes_lines(diff_lines, has_runs, merged):
    """The code-changes section of a card."""
    if has_runs:
        heading, when_empty = CHANGES_HEADING, NOTHING_CHANGED
    elif merged:
        heading, when_empty = MERGE_HEADING, MERGED_NOTE
    else:
        heading, when_empty = INITIAL_HEADING, EMPTY_PROJECT
    body = classify_diff(diff_lines)
    if not body:
        body = [StyledLine(FILE_INDENT + when_empty, KIND_NOTE)]
    return [StyledLine(heading, KIND_SECTION)] + body
@

A run that neither printed nor read anything gets no terminal section
at all.  The log spells that as the [[(no I/O)]] placeholder rather
than as an empty section (\cref{gitrepo}), and a heading above nothing
would be a heading about nothing.

<<render functions>>=
def _session_lines(run):
    """The terminal-session section of one run block."""
    io_log = run.get("io_log")
    if not io_log or io_log.strip() == NO_IO_PLACEHOLDER:
        return []
    return ([StyledLine(SESSION_HEADING, KIND_SECTION)]
            + classify_io(io_log))
@

\subsection{The way back}

Each card ends with the command that restores this version.
[[learnlog checkout]] is the one thing in the tool a student reaches
for while looking at an old run---\enquote{it worked here}---and a
reader who has to go and find out how to say it usually does not
(\cref{sec:checkout}).
The short hash is Git's own seven digits, which is what the rest of
learnlog prints and enough to be unique in a lab-sized log.

<<render functions>>=
def _checkout_hint(commit):
    """The command that brings this version back."""
    short = commit.get("hash", "")[:SHORT_HASH_LEN]
    if not short:
        return []
    return [StyledLine(
        f"To go back to this version: learnlog checkout {short}",
        KIND_META,
    )]
@

\subsection{Assembling the card}

One record, one call.
The blocks are parsed once here and their parsed forms handed to the
builders, rather than each builder re-parsing the text it needs, so a
commit holding several runs costs one parse per run and not one per
section.

[[raw]] is a parameter of this function rather than a second entry
point because both views read the same text
(\cref{sec:record-parts}).  A caller can therefore honour the flag
without knowing it exists at fetch time: it asks
[[get_commit_detail]] for Git's order once and passes whichever view
the reader chose, and a student toggling raw mode mid-playback
changes no fetch.

<<render functions>>=
def record_lines(commit, detail, *, index, total, elapsed=None,
                 tags=(), raw=False):
    """Render one record as a list of styled lines.

    ``commit`` is an entry from ``get_commits`` and ``detail``
    the output of ``get_commit_detail(..., reorder=False)``, in
    git's own order.  ``index`` is the record's zero-based
    position among ``total`` records, ``elapsed`` the seconds
    since the previous student run, and ``tags`` the tag names
    on this commit.  With ``raw`` true the same text comes back
    unchanged but coloured, instead of a card.
    """
    if raw:
        return raw_lines(detail)
    header, message, diff_lines = split_commit_detail(detail)
    <<parse the runs this commit records>>
    <<lay the card out>>
    <<close the card with the way back>>
    return lines
@

A record's subject line lives inside its own block, not in
[[commit["subject"]]]: with several blocks in one message the commit
subject is only the first of them (\cref{sec:block-sep}).
A commit that records no run has nothing but the commit subject, and
that is what the card describes.

<<parse the runs this commit records>>=
records = [
    (block.split("\n", 1)[0], parse_run_block(block))
    for block in run_blocks(message)
]
merged = any(
    line.startswith(MERGE_HEADER_PREFIX) for line in header
)
commit_meta = _commit_meta(elapsed, tags)
lines = []
@

With one run---which is every commit current learnlog writes---the card
reads as the story of that run, and the changes sit between how it went
and what it printed.
Several runs in one message mean the code did not change between them:
that is precisely why an older learnlog put them in one commit.  The
changes therefore belong to all of them equally and are shown once,
above, with a card per run underneath.

<<lay the card out>>=
if len(records) == 1:
    subject, run = records[0]
    lines.extend(_head_lines(
        commit, subject, run, index, total, commit_meta,
    ))
    lines.append(BLANK_LINE)
    lines.extend(_changes_lines(diff_lines, True, merged))
    <<append the terminal session, if there was one>>
elif records:
    lines.extend(_changes_lines(diff_lines, True, merged))
    for position, (subject, run) in enumerate(records):
        lines.append(BLANK_LINE)
        lines.extend(_head_lines(
            commit, subject, run, index, total,
            commit_meta if position == 0 else [],
        ))
        <<append the terminal session, if there was one>>
else:
    lines.extend(_head_lines(
        commit, commit.get("subject", ""), None,
        index, total, commit_meta,
    ))
    lines.append(BLANK_LINE)
    lines.extend(_changes_lines(diff_lines, False, merged))
@

Both branches that have a run append its session the same way, so the
step is written once and referenced twice---which is also what keeps
the blank line above it from being present in one layout and missing
in the other.

<<append the terminal session, if there was one>>=
session = _session_lines(run)
if session:
    lines.append(BLANK_LINE)
    lines.extend(session)
@

Every card ends the same way, whatever it described, so the hint is
appended after the branches rather than inside each of them.

<<close the card with the way back>>=
hint = _checkout_hint(commit)
if hint:
    lines.append(BLANK_LINE)
    lines.extend(hint)
@

\subsection{Verifying the card}

The fixtures below are [[git show]] output in Git's own order, which
is what a card is given.

<<render test functions>>=
def styled_text(lines):
  """The plain text of a rendered card, one line per line."""
  return "\n".join(line.prefix + line.text for line in lines)

def a_commit(**overrides):
  commit = {
    "hash": "309a26b2cad9be26f31b91455c4c3ae1ca48ec3b",
    "subject": "Run: hello.py (none)",
    "date": "2026-09-09T10:04:00+02:00",
  }
  commit.update(overrides)
  return commit

def a_detail(message, diff=None, merge=False):
  """Build git show output in git's order: message, then diff."""
  header = ["commit " + "3" * 40,
            "Author: Student <s@example.com>",
            "Date:   Wed Sep 9 16:43:55 2026 +0200"]
  if merge:
    header.insert(1, "Merge: aaaaaaa bbbbbbb")
  body = ["    " + line if line else ""
          for line in message.split("\n")]
  if diff is None:
    return "\n".join(header + [""] + body + [""])
  return "\n".join(header + [""] + body + [""] + diff + [""])

