\section{Raw mode}
\label{sec:raw-lines}

The card is an interpretation, and an interpretation can be wrong.
A teacher checking what a student's program really printed, or anyone
debugging learnlog itself, needs the bytes---so raw mode shows exactly
what [[git show]] said, in exactly its order, and adds only colour.

That constraint is worth stating as an invariant, because it is what a
test can check: for every line, [[prefix + text]] is the input line,
and joining them with newlines reproduces the input string.
Nothing here sanitises, indents, reorders or drops.  One consequence is
that a tab in a raw line reaches [[addstr]] unexpanded, where it may
cost that line; the drawer's [[curses.error]] guard is what keeps that
from being more than a missing line, and it is the price of a view
whose whole promise is fidelity.

A pleasant consequence of the invariant is that the region boundaries
need only be approximately right.  Every branch emits its line
untouched, so a message line mistaken for a diff line is drawn in the
wrong colour and in the right place---the failure mode is cosmetic, not
lossy.  It is worth knowing that, because the boundaries below are
found by the same rule the reading layer uses rather than by anything
this module reasons out for itself.

<<render constants>>=
SHOW_HEADER_PREFIXES = (
    "commit ",
    "Author:",
    "AuthorDate:",
    "Commit:",
    "CommitDate:",
    "Date:",
    "Merge:",
)
<<render functions>>=
def _raw_header_line(line):
    """Classify one line of the ``git show`` header."""
    if line.startswith(SHOW_HEADER_PREFIXES):
        return StyledLine(line, KIND_DIM)
    return StyledLine(line, KIND_PLAIN)
@

Inside the message the transcript is still worth colouring, and the
same split serves: git's four-space indent joins the stream prefix in
the faded segment, so the program's output is the coloured part of the
line here too.

<<render functions>>=
def _raw_message_line(line):
    """Classify one line of the commit message."""
    split = _io_prefix(line)
    if split is None:
        return StyledLine(line, KIND_PLAIN)
    prefix, text, kind = split
    return StyledLine(text, kind, prefix)
@

The two boundaries come from [[parse_show_sections]]
(\cref{sec:split-commit-detail}), the same pair of indices the reading
layer uses, and in Git's order they say everything there is to say:
the header ends at the first blank line, the diff begins at the first
[[diff --git]] and runs to the end, and the message is what lies
between them.
A commit with no diff---the initial one---has no second boundary, and
its message simply runs to the end instead.

Taking one layout is what makes that arithmetic honest.  Asked to
accept the diff-first text as well, this function would have to find
where a moved message begins, and the only cheap rule for that---the
first line carrying Git's four-space message indent---is wrong on
Python.  A context line inside a hunk is a space followed by the file's
own line, so a statement indented four spaces arrives as five and looks
exactly like an indented message line; the diff would end at the first
indented statement of the first hunk.  There is no such question to
answer here, because there is only one order to read.

<<render functions>>=
def raw_lines(detail):
    """Classify ``git show`` output without changing a byte.

    Expects git's own order, as returned by
    ``get_commit_detail(..., reorder=False)``.  Every line comes
    back exactly as it went in, labelled with a kind, so raw
    mode is coloured but never rewritten.
    """
    lines = detail.split("\n")
    header_end, diff_start = parse_show_sections(lines)
    message_end = len(lines) if diff_start is None else diff_start
    result = [
        _raw_header_line(line) for line in lines[:header_end + 1]
    ]
    result.extend(
        _raw_message_line(line)
        for line in lines[header_end + 1:message_end]
    )
    if diff_start is not None:
        result.extend(classify_diff(lines[diff_start:], raw=True))
    return result
@

