\section{Colour on this terminal}
\label{sec:use-color}

Whether to colour is a question about the destination, not about the
content, so it is asked once per stream rather than once per line.

The default answers it the way every well-behaved command-line tool
does.  [[NO_COLOR]] set to anything non-empty means the user has asked
for no colour anywhere;\footnote{\url{https://no-color.org/}} an empty
value is not an opt-in to the convention, so it does not disable
anything.  [[TERM=dumb]] means the terminal cannot render escapes at
all---Emacs shell buffers and some editor consoles set it.  Otherwise
we colour only a real terminal, because escape sequences in a file or a
pipe are noise in whatever reads them next.

An explicit [[--color]] beats [[NO_COLOR]], because it is the later and
more specific instruction: a user who typed it on this command line
means this command line.

Asking a stream whether it is a terminal can fail in two ways that are
both ordinary here.  A test's captured stream may not have [[isatty]]
at all, and a stream closed by a broken pipe raises [[ValueError]] on
the way out.  Neither is a reason to fail, and both mean the same
thing: nothing is watching, so do not colour.

<<render functions>>=
def use_color(stream, color):
    """Decide whether to write colour to ``stream``.

    ``color`` is ``True`` to force colour on, ``False`` to force it
    off, and ``None`` to decide from ``NO_COLOR``, ``TERM`` and
    whether ``stream`` is a terminal.
    """
    if color is not None:
        return bool(color)
    if os.environ.get("NO_COLOR"):
        return False
    if os.environ.get("TERM") == "dumb":
        return False
    try:
        return bool(stream.isatty())
    except (AttributeError, ValueError):
        return False
@

Let's verify the whole matrix: forced on, forced off, and each of the
three things automatic mode consults.

<<render test functions>>=
class FakeStream:
  def __init__(self, tty):
    self._tty = tty

  def isatty(self):
    return self._tty

def test_use_color_forced_either_way(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.setenv("NO_COLOR", "1")
  monkeypatch.setenv("TERM", "dumb")
  assert use_color(FakeStream(False), True) is True
  monkeypatch.delenv("NO_COLOR")
  monkeypatch.setenv("TERM", "xterm")
  assert use_color(FakeStream(True), False) is False

def test_use_color_auto_follows_the_terminal(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.delenv("NO_COLOR", raising=False)
  monkeypatch.setenv("TERM", "xterm")
  assert use_color(FakeStream(True), None) is True
  assert use_color(FakeStream(False), None) is False

def test_use_color_auto_obeys_no_color(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.setenv("TERM", "xterm")
  monkeypatch.setenv("NO_COLOR", "1")
  assert use_color(FakeStream(True), None) is False
  monkeypatch.setenv("NO_COLOR", "")
  assert use_color(FakeStream(True), None) is True

def test_use_color_auto_obeys_dumb_terminals(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.delenv("NO_COLOR", raising=False)
  monkeypatch.setenv("TERM", "dumb")
  assert use_color(FakeStream(True), None) is False

def test_use_color_survives_a_stream_without_isatty(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.delenv("NO_COLOR", raising=False)
  monkeypatch.setenv("TERM", "xterm")

  class Closed:
    def isatty(self):
      raise ValueError("I/O operation on closed file")

  assert use_color(object(), None) is False
  assert use_color(Closed(), None) is False
@

\subsection{Writing the escapes}

Each segment is opened and closed on its own line.
Spanning a colour across a newline is what leaves a terminal stuck in
red when the reader quits [[less]] halfway, kills a [[head]], or
watches a pipe close under a long playback; resetting at the end of
every segment costs a few bytes and makes every prefix of the output a
valid, self-contained document.

A kind whose SGR string is empty gets no escape at all, and neither
does an empty segment, so an uncoloured card is byte-for-byte the
plain text.

<<render constants>>=
SGR_RESET = "\033[0m"
<<render functions>>=
def _segment(text, kind, color):
    """One segment of a line, wrapped in its escape if any."""
    if not text or not color:
        return text
    style = STYLES.get(kind) or STYLES[KIND_PLAIN]
    if not style.sgr:
        return text
    return f"\033[{style.sgr}m{text}{SGR_RESET}"
@

An unknown kind falls back to plain rather than raising.  It should be
impossible---the table is closed and a test checks it---but a display
layer is the last thing that should stop a student from reading their
log.

<<render functions>>=
def ansi_lines(lines, color):
    """Yield each styled line as a string ending in a newline.

    With ``color`` false the plain text is yielded unchanged.
    """
    for line in lines:
        yield (_segment(line.prefix, KIND_FADED, color)
               + _segment(line.text, line.kind, color)
               + "\n")
@

Let's verify that colour is added only where it was asked for, that
each segment closes itself, and that no escape crosses a line
boundary:

<<render test functions>>=
