\section{Curses attributes}
\label{sec:curses-styles}

The viewer needs an integer per kind, and integers do not exist until
curses is running.
The [[curses]] module arrives as a parameter rather than as an import
for two reasons.  It keeps this module importable where [[curses]] is
not (\cref{viewer}), which is the whole reason \cref{sec:styled-line}
describes attributes instead of naming them.  And it lets a test pass a
stub whose colour support is deliberately broken, which is the case
that matters: the terminal that cannot do what we asked.

[[curses.wrapper]] already calls [[start_color]] inside a bare [[try]],
so calling it again costs nothing and makes the dependency visible
here, where the pairs are allocated, rather than implicit in the
caller.

Each call is guarded separately because they fail independently.
[[use_default_colors]] is the one that matters: the viewer has called
it unguarded until now, which is a crash waiting for the first
monochrome terminal---it raises on a terminal with no default-colour
support, and it is called before anything is drawn, so the whole viewer
goes down rather than losing a colour.  Guarding it means the worst
case is a viewer in bold and dim.

<<render functions>>=
def _colour_pairs(curses):
    """Allocate one colour pair per colour the table names.

    Returns a map from colour name to pair number, empty when
    the terminal has no colours.
    """
    try:
        curses.start_color()
    except curses.error:
        return {}
    try:
        curses.use_default_colors()
    except curses.error:
        pass
    try:
        if not curses.has_colors():
            return {}
    except curses.error:
        return {}
    pairs = {}
    for number, name in enumerate(_named_colours(), start=1):
        try:
            curses.init_pair(
                number, getattr(curses, "COLOR_" + name.upper()), -1,
            )
        except (curses.error, AttributeError):
            continue
        pairs[name] = number
    return pairs
@

The colours come from the style table rather than from a list of their
own, so a row that names a new colour gets a pair without anybody
remembering to add one.  Sorting makes the pair numbers deterministic,
which matters only when reading a debugger, but costs nothing.

<<render functions>>=
def _named_colours():
    """The colour names the style table uses, in a fixed order."""
    return sorted({
        style.curses.colour for style in STYLES.values()
        if style.curses.colour
    })
@

The map that comes back is complete whatever happened: a kind whose
colour could not be allocated keeps its bold, dim or reverse attribute
and loses only the colour.  A caller can therefore index it without
checking, which is what a draw loop wants.

<<render functions>>=
def curses_styles(curses):
    """Map each kind to a curses attribute.

    Takes the ``curses`` module as an argument so that this
    module stays importable where curses is not.  Colours that
    cannot be allocated degrade to bold, dim and reverse.
    """
    pairs = _colour_pairs(curses)
    attributes = {}
    for kind, style in STYLES.items():
        attribute = 0
        if style.curses.bold:
            attribute |= curses.A_BOLD
        if style.curses.dim:
            attribute |= curses.A_DIM
        if style.curses.reverse:
            attribute |= curses.A_REVERSE
        number = pairs.get(style.curses.colour)
        if number is not None:
            attribute |= curses.color_pair(number)
        attributes[kind] = attribute
    return attributes
@

Let's verify the degraded case, which is the one a student on a
monochrome terminal meets: colour support absent and every allocation
